  "title": "GlusterFS: round opaque<> dict values to the XDR 4-byte boundary",
  "description": "Closes #21364\n\n## Problem\n\n`gluster_rpc4_0_dissect_dict()` decodes `GF_DATA_TYPE_PTR` (6) dictionary values without\nrounding their length up to the XDR 4-byte boundary, unlike the `GF_DATA_TYPE_STR` (5)\nbranch, and has no case for `GF_DATA_TYPE_STR_OLD` (1), which shares the same `opaque<>`\nunion arm. When a PTR value's length is not a multiple of 4 -- e.g. the 57-byte\n`trusted.glusterfs.mdata` attribute present on nearly every LOOKUP/STAT reply once the\nctime feature is enabled (default since GlusterFS 9) -- the 1-3 XDR padding bytes are left\nunconsumed and every subsequent field is mis-aligned. The trailing `iattx` is then\ndissected at the wrong offset, so the file's size, mode, mtime, nlink, etc. come out as\ngarbage.\n\n## Fix\n\nRound the `opaque<>` value length up (matching the STR branch) and handle STR_OLD via the\nsame code path.\n\n## Verification\n\nOn a GlusterFS 11.2 capture, a 5 MiB / mode-0644 file whose LOOKUP reply previously\ndecoded as `ia_size=0, mode=0x100000` now decodes correctly (`ia_size=5242880,\nmode=0x81A4`). Also built as a downstream Debian package and confirmed on a live\nappliance testbed.\n\n`GF_DATA_TYPE_MDATA` (type 9) is also unhandled but was not observed in GlusterFS 11.2,\nwhich serializes mdata as PTR; it can be added if a capture surfaces it.\n\nBug introduced in 61aaeef2b0e (the original 4.0 dissector).\n\nAI-assisted; all changes verified against the wire format and on a live testbed.",
